course: dictionary
lesson: 3266
title: Dodging the straight answer
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Equivocation means using words in an unclear way on purpose, so you do not have to give a straight answer or tell the full truth. «I'll think about it» sounds polite and open, but if you have already decided no, it is a way of avoiding the word no.
explain: Equivocation can also be a mistake in an argument. It happens when one word is used with two different meanings. «A feather is light; what is light cannot be dark; so a feather cannot be dark.» In the first part, light means not heavy. In the second, light means bright. The word changes meaning in the middle, so the argument fails.
explain: To spot equivocation, ask two questions. Did the answer really answer the question? Did a word change its meaning partway through? If yes, you may be looking at equivocation.
fact: Equivocation is a noun: an equivocation, equivocations. It can also be used without a plural, as an uncountable noun.
fact: «Equivocation» means using unclear words on purpose to avoid a straight answer or the full truth.
fact: In logic, «equivocation» means a mistake where one word is used with two different meanings in the same argument.
fact: «Without equivocation» means clearly and directly, with no dodging.
fact: Stretching what words mean to suit yourself is called equivocation.
exercise: Mum asks, «Did you tidy your room?» Sam says, «Well, it's tidy in a way.» What is Sam doing? | Asking a question back | Making a promise | *Using unclear words to avoid a straight answer | Telling the truth without equivocation
exercise: «A feather is light; what is light cannot be dark; so a feather cannot be dark.» Which word is used with two different meanings? | dark | *light | cannot | feather
exercise: Which phrase means answering clearly, with no dodging? | *Without equivocation | Equivocation only | With equivocation | Under equivocation
test: Which one is an example of equivocation? | «Here is the book you asked for» | «I don't know the answer yet» | «Yes, I will be there at six» | *«I'll think about it», when you have already decided no
test: In the feather argument, what goes wrong? | The feather is not really light | *The word light changes its meaning halfway through | The argument has no ending | Feathers can never be dark
exam: A friend asks, «Are you coming to my party?» You say, «I'll see what I can do», meaning no. What is this? | *Equivocation, because you avoided a straight answer | A simple question about logic | A promise to come later | Honesty, because you did not lie
